# Drizzle ORM Guidelines

Core patterns and best practices for using Drizzle ORM in our PostgreSQL-based applications. These guidelines ensure type-safe, maintainable, and performant database operations.

## Core Principles
- **Type Safety** - Always leverage TypeScript and Drizzle's type system
- **SQL First** - Always prefer SQL-like query syntax over ORM abstractions
- **Performance** - Always optimize queries for PostgreSQL's strengths
- **Maintainability** - Always keep database logic clear and explicit

## Schema Definition

### Table Creation
- Always use `pgTable` for PostgreSQL tables
- Always define explicit column types
- Always add proper indexes
- Always use UUID for IDs unless there's a specific reason not to

✅ Good:
```typescript
import { pgTable, text, timestamp, uniqueIndex } from 'drizzle-orm/pg-core';

export const userTable = pgTable('user', {
  id: text('id')
    .primaryKey()
    .$defaultFn(() => crypto.randomUUID()),
    
  email: text('email').notNull(),
  name: text('name').notNull(),
  
  updatedAt: timestamp('updated_at', { mode: 'date' })
    .notNull()
    .$defaultFn(() => new Date()),
  createdAt: timestamp('created_at', { mode: 'date' })
    .notNull()
    .$defaultFn(() => new Date())
}, (table) => ({
  emailIdx: uniqueIndex('email_idx').on(table.email)
}));
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Missing types, indexes, and timestamps
const users = pgTable('users', {
  id: text('id'),
  email: text('email'),
  name: text('name')
});
```

## Query Patterns

### Select Queries
- Always use SQL-like syntax for better readability
- Always specify exact columns needed
- Always use proper joins when needed
- Always handle pagination explicitly

✅ Good:
```typescript
// Single table select with conditions
const user = await db
  .select({
    id: userTable.id,
    email: userTable.email
  })
  .from(userTable)
  .where(eq(userTable.email, email))
  .limit(1);

// Join with related data
const posts = await db
  .select({
    id: postTable.id,
    title: postTable.title,
    author: {
      id: userTable.id,
      name: userTable.name
    }
  })
  .from(postTable)
  .innerJoin(userTable, eq(postTable.authorId, userTable.id))
  .where(eq(postTable.published, true))
  .orderBy(desc(postTable.createdAt))
  .limit(10)
  .offset(page * 10);
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Using query builder abstractions
const user = await db.query.userTable.findFirst({
  where: { email }
});

// Wrong: Selecting all columns
const posts = await db
  .select()
  .from(postTable)
  .innerJoin(userTable, eq(postTable.authorId, userTable.id));
```

### Insert Operations
- Always validate data before insertion
- Always use batch inserts for multiple rows
- Always return needed data after insert

✅ Good:
```typescript
// Single insert with return value
const [user] = await db
  .insert(userTable)
  .values({
    email,
    name,
    role: 'user' as const
  })
  .returning({
    id: userTable.id,
    email: userTable.email
  });

// Batch insert
const users = await db
  .insert(userTable)
  .values(validatedUsers)
  .returning({
    id: userTable.id
  });
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Not returning inserted data
await db.insert(userTable).values(userData);

// Wrong: Multiple single inserts instead of batch
for (const user of users) {
  await db.insert(userTable).values(user);
}
```

### Update Operations
- Always use where clauses for updates
- Always validate update data
- Always return updated data when needed

✅ Good:
```typescript
const [updated] = await db
  .update(userTable)
  .set({
    name: newName,
    updatedAt: new Date()
  })
  .where(eq(userTable.id, userId))
  .returning({
    id: userTable.id,
    name: userTable.name
  });
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Missing where clause
await db.update(userTable).set(data);

// Wrong: Not returning updated data when needed
await db
  .update(userTable)
  .set(data)
  .where(eq(userTable.id, id));
```

### Delete Operations
- Always use where clauses for deletes
- Always consider soft deletes for important data
- Always handle cascading deletes explicitly

✅ Good:
```typescript
// Hard delete with where clause
const [deleted] = await db
  .delete(userTable)
  .where(eq(userTable.id, userId))
  .returning({
    id: userTable.id
  });

// Soft delete
const [deactivated] = await db
  .update(userTable)
  .set({
    deletedAt: new Date(),
    updatedAt: new Date()
  })
  .where(eq(userTable.id, userId))
  .returning({
    id: userTable.id
  });
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Delete without where clause
await db.delete(userTable);

// Wrong: Not handling cascading deletes
await db
  .delete(userTable)
  .where(eq(userTable.id, userId));
```

### Error Handling
- Always use transactions for multi-table operations
- Always handle unique constraint violations
- Always validate data before database operations

✅ Good:
```typescript
try {
  const [user] = await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    const [user] = await tx
      .insert(userTable)
      .values(userData)
      .returning();
      
    await tx
      .insert(profileTable)
      .values({
        userId: user.id,
        ...profileData
      });
      
    return [user];
  });
} catch (error) {
  if (error.code === '23505') { // Unique violation
    throw new AppError('#ERR_DUPLICATE', 409);
  }
  throw error;
}
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: No transaction for multi-table operation
const user = await db.insert(userTable).values(userData);
await db.insert(profileTable).values({ userId: user.id });

// Wrong: Not handling database errors
try {
  await db.insert(userTable).values(userData);
} catch (e) {
  throw e;
}
```
